1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,750
In 1018, at a banquet in the capital, a man recited a poem.

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:10,040
This world is mine, he said, like the full moon with nothing missing.

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:11,830
He wasn't the emperor.

4
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:14,050
He was the emperor's grandfather.

5
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:17,170
Today we're in the Heian period.

6
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:21,120
It begins in 794, when the capital moved to Heian-kyo,

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,390
and it lasts about four hundred years.

8
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:25,340
Let me give you the answer first.

9
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:26,930
For those four hundred years,

10
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:31,020
the person really running the country was not the emperor himself.

11
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:33,390
It was the emperor's grandfather.

12
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:35,940
First a grandfather on the mother's side, then

13
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:37,900
one on the father's side, and then

14
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:40,970
once again, a grandfather on the mother's side.

15
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:43,530
The face in that seat changed three times.

16
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:47,470
Today, we'll read the Heian period through those three grandfathers.

17
00:00:48,470 --> 00:00:49,990
First, a new capital.

18
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:54,170
Heian-kyo, today's Kyoto, was founded by Emperor Kanmu.

19
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:58,040
In Nara, the temples had meddled too much in politics,

20
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:02,320
so the new capital allowed no temples except To-ji and Sai-ji,

21
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,330
the two guarding its main gate.

22
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:06,330
Buddhism moved out to the mountains.

23
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:11,740
In 806, Saicho founded the Tendai school on Mount Hiei, and in 816,

24
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:14,730
Kukai founded the Shingon school on Mount Koya.

25
00:01:15,730 --> 00:01:17,250
The first grandfather.

26
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:20,220
The first grandfather was the Fujiwara family.

27
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:24,530
Marry a daughter to the emperor, and make her son the next emperor.

28
00:01:24,530 --> 00:01:29,060
While the emperor is still a child, his grandfather governs in his place.

29
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:31,070
That role is called regent.

30
00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:33,660
In 866, Fujiwara no Yoshifusa

31
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:36,740
became the first regent from outside the imperial family.

32
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:40,730
Around this time, in 894, the scholar Sugawara

33
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:44,190
no Michizane proposed stopping the official missions to Tang

34
00:01:44,190 --> 00:01:47,550
China, and contact with China grew thin.

35
00:01:47,550 --> 00:01:52,620
As a result, a culture of Japan's own grew, including the kana script.

36
00:01:52,620 --> 00:01:56,880
Michizane himself was later driven out of the capital by the Fujiwara.

37
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,300
The family reached its peak with Fujiwara

38
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:02,780
no Michinaga, the man with the full-moon poem.

39
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:05,500
He became the grandfather of three emperors,

40
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:10,800
and his son Yorimichi served as regent and chief adviser for about fifty years.

41
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:13,830
But Yorimichi's daughter never gave birth to a prince.

42
00:02:13,830 --> 00:02:16,890
If you can't become the grandfather, the whole system stops.

43
00:02:16,890 --> 00:02:19,820
Meanwhile, rebellions broke out far from the capital.

44
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:24,330
In 935, Taira no Masakado rose in the Kanto region,

45
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:26,440
and around the same time, Fujiwara

46
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:29,380
no Sumitomo rose on the Seto Inland Sea.

47
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:31,120
The warrior families who put them down,

48
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,790
the Taira and the Minamoto, grew stronger.

49
00:02:34,790 --> 00:02:36,230
The second grandfather.

50
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:39,250
Once an emperor came along without Fujiwara blood,

51
00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:42,620
the imperial family moved to take politics back.

52
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:47,060
In 1086, Emperor Shirakawa handed the throne to his son,

53
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:51,130
became a retired emperor, and kept running the government anyway.

54
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:53,230
This is called cloistered rule.

55
00:02:53,230 --> 00:02:55,870
He kept governing into his grandson's reign.

56
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:58,330
Picture a CEO who steps down,

57
00:02:58,330 --> 00:03:01,200
then keeps running the company as chairman of the board.

58
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:03,580
The third grandfather.

59
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:06,030
The warriors who guarded the retired emperors

60
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:09,190
soon became the stars of the court's own fights.

61
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:14,900
Through the Hogen Rebellion in 1156 and the Heiji Rebellion in 1159, Taira

62
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:18,520
no Kiyomori defeated Minamoto no Yoshitomo.

63
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:21,860
Kiyomori rose all the way to Grand Minister of State

64
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:24,290
and married his daughter to the emperor.

65
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:27,230
Their son became Emperor Antoku.

66
00:03:27,230 --> 00:03:31,710
Kiyomori had taken the same seat the Fujiwara once held: the mother's father.

67
00:03:31,710 --> 00:03:33,420
Kiyomori wasn't so much the man

68
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:37,740
who opened a new age as the man who copied the Heian way better than anyone.

69
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:41,740
And that's exactly why he drew the same resentment the nobles had.

70
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:44,380
In 1180, members of the imperial family

71
00:03:44,380 --> 00:03:47,000
and the Minamoto rose up against the Taira,

72
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,120
and in 1185, the Taira were destroyed at Dan-no-ura.

73
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:56,540
The Fujiwara, the retired emperors, and Taira no Kiyomori.

74
00:03:56,540 --> 00:03:58,880
As these three grandfathers took turns,

75
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:01,760
the Heian period moved through its four hundred years.

76
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:04,230
And the ones who finally took the seat were warriors

77
00:04:04,230 --> 00:04:06,670
who never needed to be anyone's grandfather.

78
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:08,320
Here's a question for you.

79
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:11,140
If you were a powerful noble in Heian Japan,

80
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:14,690
would you rather be the emperor, or the emperor's grandfather?

81
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:15,970
Tell me in the comments.

82
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:18,560
If you enjoyed this, give it a like and subscribe,

83
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:20,410
so you don't miss the next episode.

84
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:21,980
Next time: the Genpei War.

85
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:26,110
We'll follow the six years until the Taira fell, through four challengers.

